ntdll: Reuse partially freed LFH groups.

A full LFH group is detached from its bin and only goes back to it once all
of its blocks are freed. A program that allocates many small blocks and frees
most but not all of them keeps every such group, and the freed blocks in it are
never used again. Backblaze's bztransmit allocates about 31 small strings per
file record and keeps 3 of them: backing up 15 million files, a heap walk found
17.5 GiB of freed blocks inside groups holding 4.2 GiB of used ones, and the
process used 27 GB of memory.

Link a group back to its bin as soon as one of its blocks is freed, and
allocate from partially used groups first, keeping two empty groups per bin and
releasing the others. A per-bin spin lock protects the groups list and their
free bits, replacing the lock-free list and the per-thread affinity groups.
Nothing else is acquired while it is held, so HeapLock still does not block LFH
allocations from other threads.

Also:
- size LFH blocks by their size class instead of HEAP_MIN_BLOCK_SIZE, which
  only non-LFH blocks need for their free list entry, so a request of up to 24
  bytes takes 32 bytes instead of 48, as on Windows;
- discard the pages of free blocks of 256 KiB or more in the middle of a
  subheap with MEM_RESET, so that freed memory goes back to the system;
- keep the block's own size when an LFH block is shrunk in place. The size
  class of the new size was used instead, so HeapSize returned a wrong size
  after shrinking a block that was in a larger bin.

